<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabyles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kabyle people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyles</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, a mountainous region east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> that includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djurdjura">Djurdjura</a> range. Speaking the Kabyle language, they lived in dense village communities governed by assemblies and long kept their autonomy from Ottoman Algiers. They led the Mokrani revolt of 1871 against French rule and played a large part in Algeria's war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabyles.jpg" alt="Kabyles" width="314" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WomanOfKabyleXIX-haik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> comes from the Arabic qaba'il, meaning tribes, a word once used of mountain-dwelling peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> generally. For the Kabyles specifically the older name is Zwawa, used since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> for the tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-kabylia">Greater Kabylia</a> and found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>. According to Mohamed Benrabah, the French first used Kabyle for all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and only in the colonial era narrowed it to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a>. Their own names for the region are Tamurt Idurar, Land of Mountains, and Tamurt n Iqbayliyen, Land of the Kabyle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyles</a> were among the few peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> who stayed independent under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and Ottoman Turks, and even after the Arab conquest they kept their mountains. Between 902 and 909 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-kabylia">Little Kabylia</a> helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>. The Zirids, a family of Sanhaja Berbers from the Kabyle mountains, later ruled much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, and the Hammadids, who broke from them, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> and other cities.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyles</a> lived mainly in three entities: the Kingdom of Kuku, the Kingdom of Ait Abbas and the principality of Aït Jubar. Kabylia was the last part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> taken by the French, in 1854 to 1857, despite vigorous resistance, and leaders such as Lalla Fatma N'Soumer fought on until Mokrani's rebellion of 1871. The French took land from resisting tribes for colonists and deported many resisters, mainly to New Caledonia. Many Kabyles emigrated, including as workers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where Algerian migrants in the 1920s organised the first party for independence.</p><p>After independence in 1962 tensions with the central government recurred. Hocine Aït Ahmed founded the Socialist Forces Front in September 1963, and the army was sent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a>; about 400 deaths were counted among the maquis. In 1980 months of protest, called the Berber Spring, demanded recognition of Berber as an official language. In 2001 the killing of a student, Massinissa Guermah, in custody set off the Black Spring, in which at least 126 people were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Tamazight</a> was recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s constitution on 6 January 2016.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> villages were governed by assemblies called djemaas, which settled disputes and set customary law and which French administration preserved as part of an indirect system. The Kabyles speak Kabyle, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> language of the Afroasiatic family, spoken by about 3 million people in 2004. It was once written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script">Libyco-Berber script</a>, and since the 19th century in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet. They are mainly Muslim, with a small Christian minority. Their economy has rested on orchards, olive trees and crafts such as pottery and tapestry, and oral poetry, the asefru, is performed by imusnawen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabyle people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moriscos expelled from Spain, Jewish merchants, <b>Kabyle</b> labourers and Arab and Berber townspeople made up the rest of the urban population, while the countryside remained largely tribal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's rulers drew on <b>Kabyle</b> tribal levies armed with firearms and on the craft workshops of the Kalâa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ait-abbas">Kingdom of Ait Abbas</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors ruled the <b>Kabyle</b> highlands, sometimes allied with the Regency of Algiers, sometimes with Spain, and competed with the Kingdom of Ait Abbas.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kuku">Kingdom of Kuku</a></li></ul></section>
